edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186240135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240770 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fbad1630-befd-5b0d-9389-d98b46343814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240777 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240777 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240777 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240777 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186240311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240777 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186240326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240777 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240777 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240777 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186240311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240777 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe8d7464-8b34-59c0-a3ab-f6d1c3d9d9bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240918 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240918 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104725" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240918 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240918 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(3{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2x)\\,i+(3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4y)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240918 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "227ca00b-2249-5c8a-8773-a9971e9ef701" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240918 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,e)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({1}^{3}\\cdot {e}^{3}+{1}^{2}-2\\cdot {e}^{2})-({0}^{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}+{0}^{2}-2\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{3}+1-2{e}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={e}^{3}-2{e}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241046 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241046 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241046 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241046 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$ \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,g(t)=15t+3\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\\}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{1}(t)\\}$ equals $15t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241046 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c464809f-b653-57ad-9d07-cbfb8f516b25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241046 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the set $\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=15t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{2}(t)\\}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(15t+3)\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}15{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{15}{4}{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{15}{4}+1)-(\\frac{15}{4}-1) \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{f}_{2}(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{5}{t}^{5}|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}-(-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{2}{(\\frac{2}{5})}\\cdot {t}^{2}=5{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =15t+5{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241104 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241104 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241104 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+4{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25}=1.$ What is the second component of $f?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241104 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb2247f7-5823-54d1-ba7e-d63c871dccbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241104 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=2X,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}Y,\\,z=5Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2X,\\frac{1}{2}Y,5Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250989 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250989 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250989 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "34813407-d25e-5329-a49d-90dd01f166fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251576 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251576 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251576 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7b123f7b-16ce-5745-8c7f-29a3af037f52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251604 #edb/ref 1080 "2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251604 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251604 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7da8a500-b479-5c6c-814e-26884a69c42f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251761 #edb/ref 1080 "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251761 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251761 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e807c03-b5d6-572a-8190-75f1c2889bc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252488 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252488 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252488 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dc04463b-6bd3-537d-b8a6-0f5c1de23fda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186250989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186252488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252946 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252946 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252946 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186251761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252946 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "516c4e1a-8e61-51a8-bdbd-a5bccc229947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253323 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253323 #edb/ref 1069 "q-117278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253323 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253323 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(1)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253323 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c2ae5d5-ee50-5ac5-adef-e81c842f8f25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253323 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{a}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $-{x}^{3}$ and $x.$\n\nThe integrand ${t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(-3{x}^{2}){u}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{(-{x}^{3})}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{x}^{3})+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{x}^{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=-{x}^{3}.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{1}^{3}+{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡1 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256802 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{10}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256802 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256802 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "021c19b6-a87f-59bb-986b-654c56b918a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256839 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{6}-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256839 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256839 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06c9589f-c628-5f4a-a78a-70eef556a95c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256862 #edb/ref 1080 "12{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-6t+9)\\,\\text{d}t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256862 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256862 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "091b6b7e-813c-5b73-865c-f669e169c171" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256876 #edb/ref 1080 "x{2}^{x}=x+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){x}^{2}+\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}{x}^{3}}{2}+⋯" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256876 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256876 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a4a297e-b4a6-5cad-93c5-88029bba263f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256893 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256893 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256893 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0bb8367f-a159-5457-99f5-50133f64eb7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256925 #edb/ref 1080 "3e+1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256925 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256925 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0eea1d61-c31a-52a0-9c80-b705eeabdf54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256930 #edb/ref 108